   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australian Red Cross - Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (DFAT) Partnership 2019-2024 Mid-term Review</narrative>
   </title>
   <category code="A07"></category>
   <language code="EN"></language>
   <document-date iso-date="2023-01-01"></document-date>
   <dfat:recipient-region code="998"></dfat:recipient-region>
  </document-link>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening regional architecture</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia contributed to strengthened shelter cluster coordination in the Pacific region through the revision of Shelter Cluster terms of reference in Fiji, technical guidance on standardised emergency shelter and essential household items assistance in Tonga, and support for the coordination of three tropical cyclones in Vanuatu.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported Red Cross Red Crescent-led technical assistance for the development of more effective disaster laws and policies in the Pacific, including the integration of climate, anticipatory action and public health emergencies into regional disaster management approaches, guidelines, and agreements. Key initiatives included co-chairing the Technical Working Group on Risk Governance and Resilient Development under the Framework for Resilient Development in the Pacific, technical support for the annual Pacific Anticipatory Action Week, which brings together state and civil society leaders from Pacific Island Countries and regional partners, and leading the technical sub-working group on Institutionalising Anticipatory Action into Law and Policy in Asia Pacific.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported the training of community members to prevent, detect and take prompt local actions to control communicable disease threats. Through the Red Cross Red Crescent toolkit of Epidemic Control for Volunteers, communities in Timor-Leste and Vanuatu gained awareness of how communicable diseases are spread and how to prevent and control outbreaks, particularly relating to dengue, leptospirosis, malaria and diarrhoea. In Mongolia, support has extend to include community-based surveillance (CBS) with a One Health approach in collaboration with the Mongolia Centre for Zoonotic Diseases (CZD. Communities have been trained to detect and notify Government surveillance through real-time mobile phone SMS systems and take disease control actions relating to humans, animals and the environment.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australian Red Cross held two Disaster Law workshops in August and September 2022 to facilitate peer-to-peer exchanges focusing on the auxiliary role of National Societies and experience in disaster legislative review processes. Fiji Red Cross supported National Societies in the Pacific by sharing its experiences of engagement in legislative and policy processes. Plans were developed to support similar regional peer exchanges with Mongolian Red Cross and National Societies in Asia.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social protection measures</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australian Red Cross supported human resource capacity in IFRC and Vanuatu Red Cross to advance the Pacific Cash Readiness Roadmap and contribute to integration of cash and voucher assistance into the disaster preparedness plans and operational procedures of Vanuatu Red Cross. Vanuatu Red Cross piloted community cash and voucher assistance which disbursed unconditional cash to 135 recipients (70 female, 65 male; including 8 female, 10 male persons with disabilities) in Port Vila. In March 2023, after TC Judy and Kevin devastated Vanuatu, VRCS provided cash and voucher assistance, to Port Vila and remote islands in Vanuatu (see INO204).</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Fiji Red Cross scaled their community-based surveillance program to 201 epidemic-prone &#x2018;hot spot&#x2019; communities in Northern and Western Divisions. Trained volunteers (186 females and 15 males) have remained engaged and are working closely with zone nurses and health inspectors. On average, 85% of volunteers reported weekly across the year, and follow up of these reports indicate they have identified cases of COVID-19, typhoid, leptospirosis and dengue. Volunteers and health authorities took timely action as a result of these reports to avert further transmission in affected communities.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Following the Indonesian Government&apos;s declaration of a polio outbreak in Aceh in November 2022, Pelan Merah Indonesia (PMI, Indonesian Red Cross) collaborated with the Ministry of Health and other persons to vaccinate more than 95,000 children. In three weeks, PMI volunteers visited 2,205 households in the Pidie sub-district, raising awareness about vaccination and hygiene to prevent the disease. Volunteers conducted community surveillance and reported paralysis, a key symptom of polio. This first phase of the immunisation campaign achieved 92.4% coverage in Pidie District. At the provincial level, the campaign achieved first round polio vaccine coverage of 93.7% in Aceh.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2024, Australia provided core funding to nine National Society partners (Fiji, Papua New Guinea, Solomon Islands, Tonga, Vanuatu, Indonesia, Mongolia, Myanmar and Timor-Leste) through the ARC-DFAT Partnership. Investment in governance and management included support to strengthen frameworks that underpin the National Societies&#x2019; auxiliary role, strategic planning, financial management systems, human resources, volunteer management, and domestic resource mobilisation. Over the life of the Partnership, the proportion of funds provided directly to National Societies increased, reflecting improved partner capacity. Through the Partnership, over 30 disaster law-related publications have been produced, influencing disaster risk policy and practice. Investment in strong governance has led to strong local organisations and effective disaster responders in partner National Societies</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Assistance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2024, Australian funding supported the International Federation of the Red Cross Disaster Response Emergency Fund (IFRC DREF) to enable Red Cross and Red Crescent National Societies to assist around 3 million people affected by 33 crises and disasters in the Asia and Pacific regions. Immediately following the Vanuatu earthquake in December 2024, the IFRC DREF released funding to Vanuatu Red Cross. This enabled VRCS to undertake needs assessments and deliver essential shelter to 4640 people, reach 9663 people with emergency Water, Sanitation and Health (WASH) relief, and provide Community Protection, Gender and Inclusion (PGI) awareness and training to 659 people. Australia complemented the DREF funding with additional funding direct to the National Society.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency assistance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Partnership supported improved accountability to affected people and greater social inclusion during responses. For example, PNG Red Cross with the support of a short-term Community Engagement and Accountability delegate, trained 80 staff and volunteers who conducted a household survey which gathered insights into the experiences of communities during the COVID-19 pandemic, including the challenges and vulnerabilities they faced. In Timor-Leste, relief items tailored to the needs of groups facing protection risks were provided to 146 pregnant and lactating mothers (including 81 baby kits, 4 hygiene kits), 257 older persons, and 83 persons with disabilities.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
